**European Parliament resolution on Same person, different social media personas**

**The European Parliament,**

-- having regard to Articles 2 and 3 of the Treaty on European Union on the Union’s values and objectives,

-- having regard to Articles 8, 16, and 21 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union on data protection, internal market freedoms, and non-discrimination,

-- having regard to Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (General Data Protection Regulation),

-- having regard to Regulation (EU) 2022/2065 (Digital Services Act),

-- having regard to Regulation (EU) 2021/784 on addressing the dissemination of terrorist content online,

-- having regard to Directive (EU) 2018/1808 on audiovisual media services,

-- having regard to the European Commission’s 2020 European Democracy Action Plan,

-- having regard to the European Commission’s 2021 Guidance on Strengthening the Code of Practice on Disinformation,

-- having regard to the European Parliament resolution of 20 May 2021 on the Digital Services Act and Digital Markets Act,

-- having regard to Rule 132(2) of its Rules of Procedure,

A.  whereas social media platforms have become central to public and private life, influencing democratic discourse, economic activity, and personal expression; whereas individuals increasingly curate multiple online personas across different platforms, reflecting diverse professional, social, and emotional needs;

B.  whereas the phenomenon of fragmented online identities—such as being professional on LinkedIn, expressive on Instagram, humorous on TikTok, opinionated on X (formerly Twitter), and inactive on Facebook—reflects both the opportunities and pressures of digital communication in a hyper-connected society;

C.  whereas such fragmentation may contribute to mental health challenges, including anxiety, identity confusion, and digital fatigue, as individuals navigate different social expectations and content moderation regimes;

D.  whereas the absence of a unified regulatory framework for online identity and content moderation across platforms creates inconsistencies in user protection and accountability;

E.  whereas the European Union has affirmed the right to data protection (Article 8 Charter of Fundamental Rights) and the right to freedom of expression (Article 11 Charter), both of which are implicated when users manage multiple digital identities;

F.  whereas the Digital Services Act (DSA) introduces new obligations for transparency, risk mitigation, and user empowerment, yet does not explicitly address the psychological or social implications of fragmented online personas;

G.  whereas algorithmic amplification of content across platforms can reinforce polarisation, misinformation, and emotional extremes, particularly on platforms such as X and TikTok;

H.  whereas the lack of interoperability between platforms limits users’ ability to control their digital footprint or maintain a coherent online presence;

I.  whereas the European Commission has emphasised the need to promote digital literacy and online resilience, including through the Digital Education Action Plan;

J.  whereas the phenomenon raises ethical questions about authenticity, accountability, and the commercial exploitation of personal data under the guise of personal expression;

1.  Calls on the European Commission to analyse the social, psychological, and democratic impacts of fragmented online identities and to assess whether existing EU legislation adequately protects users in such contexts;

2.  Urges the Commission to consider amendments to the Digital Services Act and future digital legislation to include provisions on digital identity coherence, user control over cross-platform personas, and transparency in algorithmic content curation;

3.  Condemns the exploitation of users’ emotional states and identity fragmentation by platforms through manipulative design and engagement-driven algorithms;

4.  Expresses concern that the current lack of harmonised rules on online identity management may exacerbate mental health issues and polarisation;

5.  Welcomes the inclusion of digital literacy in the Digital Education Action Plan and calls for enhanced support for mental health awareness in digital education programmes;

6.  Stresses that any future regulation must balance the protection of freedom of expression with the need to safeguard users from harm arising from fragmented or curated online behaviour;

7.  Requests the Commission to develop guidelines for platforms on ethical design practices that reduce the psychological burden on users managing multiple personas;

8.  Emphasises the importance of interoperability standards that allow users to transfer, archive, or integrate their digital identities across platforms with full consent and data portability;

9.  Proposes that the European Digital Media Observatory be tasked with monitoring trends in online identity fragmentation and its societal impacts, reporting annually to Parliament and Council;

10.  Reiterates the need for robust enforcement of the DSA, including penalties for platforms that fail to address systemic risks linked to identity fragmentation and emotional manipulation;

11.  Instructs its President to forward this resolution to the Council, the Commission, the Vice-President of the Commission / High Representative of the Union for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy, and the governments and parliaments of the Member States.

**European Parliament resolution on Local producers are important!**

**The European Parliament,**

-- having regard to Articles 2 and 3 of the Treaty on European Union,

-- having regard to Articles 179 to 190 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union,

-- having regard to Regulation (EU) 2024/... on the Common Agricultural Policy,

-- having regard to Regulation (EU) 2024/... on the internal market for agricultural and food products,

-- having regard to its resolution of 15 January 2024 on the promotion of local food chains,

-- having regard to the Commission communication of 10 March 2024 on supporting local producers in the EU,

-- having regard to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals, particularly Goal 2 on zero hunger,

-- having regard to Rule 132(2) of its Rules of Procedure,

A.  whereas local producers play a crucial role in ensuring food security, preserving cultural heritage, and promoting sustainable development in the European Union;

B.  whereas local producers face significant challenges, including competition from large-scale producers, market access barriers, and the impacts of climate change;

C.  whereas supporting local producers aligns with the EU's objectives of promoting rural development, sustainable agriculture, and short food supply chains;

D.  whereas local producers contribute to the economic vitality of rural areas and help maintain the diversity of agricultural landscapes;

E.  whereas consumers increasingly demand locally produced, high-quality, and sustainable food products;

F.  whereas the COVID-19 pandemic has highlighted the importance of resilient and diversified food supply chains;

G.  whereas the EU has set ambitious targets for sustainable agriculture and rural development in the framework of the European Green Deal;

H.  whereas targeted support measures can help local producers to innovate, adapt to climate change, and meet consumer demands;

1.  Calls on the Commission to propose targeted measures to support local producers, including financial assistance, capacity building, and market access initiatives;

2.  Urges Member States to integrate support for local producers into their national strategic plans under the Common Agricultural Policy;

3.  Welcomes the Commission's recognition of the importance of local producers in its communication of 10 March 2024;

4.  Stresses that supporting local producers is essential for achieving the EU's goals of sustainable agriculture, rural development, and food security;

5.  Requests the Commission to assess the impact of existing policies on local producers and to propose amendments where necessary to better support them;

6.  Emphasises the need for increased cooperation between local producers, consumers, and other stakeholders to promote short food supply chains;

7.  Invites the Commission to explore the possibility of establishing a dedicated EU fund to support local producers in their efforts to innovate and adapt to climate change;

8.  Instructs its President to forward this resolution to the Council, the Commission, the Vice-President of the Commission / High Representative of the Union for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy, the governments and parliaments of the Member States, and any other relevant addressees.

**European Parliament resolution on Agrifood is good**

**The European Parliament,**

-- having regard to Articles 2 and 3 of the Treaty on European Union,

-- having regard to Articles 42 and 43 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union,

-- having regard to Regulation (EU) 2024/... on the common organisation of the markets in agricultural products,

-- having regard to Regulation (EU) 2024/... on the common agricultural policy,

-- having regard to its resolution of 12 March 2024 on the promotion of local food cultures in the EU,

-- having regard to the Commission communication of 15 January 2024 on the future of the EU agrifood sector,

-- having regard to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals, in particular Goal 2 on zero hunger,

-- having regard to Rule 132(2) of its Rules of Procedure,

A.  whereas the EU agrifood sector is a vital component of the European economy, employing around 44 million people and contributing significantly to the Union's GDP;

B.  whereas the agrifood sector is deeply rooted in local cultures and traditions, with each Member State boasting a unique culinary heritage;

C.  whereas the promotion of local food cultures can enhance rural development, preserve biodiversity, and strengthen food security;

D.  whereas the EU has set ambitious targets for sustainable agriculture and food production, as outlined in the Farm to Fork Strategy;

E.  whereas the COVID-19 pandemic has highlighted the importance of resilient and diversified food supply chains;

F.  whereas the EU must strike a balance between promoting local food cultures and ensuring fair competition within the single market;

G.  whereas consumers are increasingly interested in the origin and production methods of their food, driving demand for local and traditional products;

H.  whereas the EU has a range of tools at its disposal to support local food cultures, including quality schemes such as Protected Designation of Origin (PDO), Protected Geographical Indication (PGI), and Traditional Speciality Guaranteed (TSG);

I.  whereas the EU must address the challenges faced by local producers, including market access, competition from imported products, and the impacts of climate change;

J.  whereas the EU's Common Agricultural Policy (CAP) plays a crucial role in supporting farmers and rural communities, and should be further leveraged to promote local food cultures;

1.  Calls on the Commission to develop a comprehensive strategy to promote and protect local food cultures across the EU, building on existing quality schemes and initiatives;

2.  Urges the Commission to facilitate market access for local producers, including through the promotion of short food supply chains and direct sales;

3.  Calls for the strengthening of consumer information and education on the importance of local food cultures and the benefits of purchasing local products;

4.  Expresses concern about the impacts of climate change on local food production and stresses the need for targeted support to help farmers adapt and mitigate these effects;

5.  Welcomes the Commission's Farm to Fork Strategy and its recognition of the importance of sustainable food production and consumption;

6.  Stresses that the promotion of local food cultures should be fully aligned with the EU's sustainability objectives, including the reduction of food waste and the promotion of healthy diets;

7.  Requests the Commission to assess the potential for further integration of local food cultures into the EU's education and training programmes, including vocational education and training for farmers and food producers;

8.  Emphasises the importance of interregional cooperation and knowledge exchange to promote local food cultures and foster innovation in the agrifood sector;

9.  Calls on Member States to make full use of the CAP's tools and funding opportunities to support local food cultures and rural development;

10.  Instructs its President to forward this resolution to the Council, the Commission, the Vice-President of the Commission / High Representative of the Union for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy, the governments and parliaments of the Member States, and any other relevant addressees.

**European Parliament resolution on Agrifood local producers**

**The European Parliament,**

-- having regard to Articles 38-44 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union (TFEU), concerning the common agricultural policy,

-- having regard to Regulation (EU) No 1308/2013 establishing a common organisation of the markets in agricultural products,

-- having regard to the European Green Deal, and the Farm to Fork Strategy, as set out in the Commission Communication of 20 May 2020,

-- having regard to the European Parliament resolution of 25 October 2018 on the future of food and farming,

-- having regard to the Charter of Fundamental Rights of the European Union, in particular Article 16 on the freedom to conduct a business in a way that respects sustainable development,

-- having regard to Rule 132(2) of its Rules of Procedure,

-- having regard to the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals, particularly Goal 2 on Zero Hunger and Goal 12 on Responsible Consumption and Production,

-- having regard to the International Treaty on Plant Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture,

-- having regard to the Paris Agreement on climate change, and the need for sustainable practices in all sectors, including agriculture,

A.  whereas local agrifood producers play a crucial role in the sustainability and resilience of food systems in Europe;

B.  whereas these producers face significant challenges, including market access, competition with large-scale industrial producers, and the impacts of climate change;

C.  whereas local agrifood production is aligned with the objectives of the European Green Deal and the Farm to Fork Strategy, contributing to sustainable and fair food systems;

D.  whereas the promotion and support of local agrifood producers can enhance food security, preserve local biodiversity, and strengthen rural economies;

E.  whereas there is a growing consumer demand for locally produced, high-quality, and sustainable food products;

F.  whereas the COVID-19 pandemic has highlighted the importance of resilient and diversified food supply chains;

G.  whereas small-scale and family farmers often lack the resources and knowledge to navigate the complexities of EU regulations and market requirements;

H.  whereas digitalisation and innovation offer significant opportunities for local agrifood producers to improve productivity and sustainability;

I.  whereas the protection of geographical indications and traditional specialities guarantees the quality of local agrifood products and supports rural development;

J.  whereas the empowerment and support of local agrifood producers are essential for achieving the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals;

1.  Calls on the Commission to propose legislation aimed at improving market access for local agrifood producers and ensuring fair competition within the internal market;

2.  Urges Member States to implement targeted support programmes for local agrifood producers, focusing on innovation, sustainability, and digitalisation;

3.  Expresses support for the development of EU-wide initiatives to promote the consumption of locally produced agrifood products among consumers;

4.  Welcomes the efforts made under the European Green Deal and the Farm to Fork Strategy to create a more sustainable food system and stresses the importance of integrating local agrifood producers in these efforts;

5.  Stresses the need for simplified EU regulations and administrative procedures to facilitate the participation of small-scale and family farmers in EU agrifood markets;

6.  Requests the Commission to enhance the protection of geographical indications and traditional specialities as a means to support local agrifood producers;

7.  Emphasises the importance of research and innovation in supporting the sustainability and competitiveness of local agrifood producers;

8.  Proposes the creation of an EU platform for the exchange of best practices among local agrifood producers, aiming at improving sustainability, productivity, and market access;

9.  Underlines the need for EU policies and programmes to include specific measures that address the challenges faced by local agrifood producers in the context of climate change and biodiversity loss;

10.  Recommends the inclusion of local agrifood producers in the planning and implementation of national and regional strategies for rural development, ensuring that their needs and potential contributions to sustainable development are fully recognised;

11.  Instructs its President to forward this resolution to the Council, the Commission, the governments and parliaments of the Member States.
